UID:430cbdd076d2ad80@fed-dol-news.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261123T235057Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261123
SUMMARY:[Federal Practice Areas] US Department of Labor proposes to update
  fiduciary rule to affirm Employee Retirement Income Security Act’s excl
 usive purpose rule
DESCRIPTION:WASHINGTON – The U.S. Department of Labor’s Employee Benef
 its Security Administration today issued proposed amendments to the invest
 ment duties regulation to clarify the fiduciary duty of loyalty under the 
 Employee Retirement Income Security Act.The proposed rule seeks to end the
  32-year uncertainty surrounding whether ERISA allows fiduciaries to consi
 der non-financial factors in retirement plan investments\, including envir
 onmental\, social\, and governance\, and diversity\, equity\, and inclusio
 n. The proposal gives fiduciaries an affirmative expression of their duty 
 of loyalty\, which would clarify that ERISA requires plan fiduciaries to a
 ct solely in the interest of the plan’s participants and beneficiaries\,
  and for the exclusive purpose of providing for the interests of participa
 nts and beneficiaries in their retirement income or financial benefits und
 er the plan.“President Trump’s Department of Labor is not putting its 
 thumb on the scale for or against any particular investment or asset class
 \,” said U.S. Secretary of Labor Keith Sonderling. “Our proposed rule 
 makes clear that retirement plan fiduciaries must make investment decision
 s solely in the financial interests of workers and retirees\, not to advan
 ce political or social agendas. Our focus is on protecting and growing Ame
 ricans’ retirement savings\, which have reached record levels thanks to 
 President Trump.”If finalized\, the proposal would also place a heighten
 ed focus on shareholder engagement and proxy voting by fiduciaries to secu
 re the retirement interests of American workers and would avoid the improp
 er pressures and influence placed on plan fiduciaries. This includes votin
 g shares and otherwise attempting to influence corporate behavior through 
 shareholder engagement activities for ESG/DEI purposes. The proposed rule 
 follows the directive in President Trump’s executive order\, “Protecti
 ng American Investors from Foreign-Owned and Politically Motivated Proxy A
 dvisors\,” which instructed the department to stre\nhttp://www.dol.gov/n
 ewsroom/releases/ebsa/ebsa20261123
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/ebsa/ebsa20261123
CATEGORIES:Federal Practice Areas
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:c5d72888ca2b0024@fed-dol-news.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261123T172614Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261119
SUMMARY:[Federal Practice Areas] US Department of Labor announces final ru
 le clarifying employee\, independent contractor status under federal wage 
 and hour laws
DESCRIPTION:WASHINGTON – The U.S. Department of Labor’s Wage and Hour
  Division today announced a final rule to revise its analysis for disting
 uishing between employees and independent contractors under the Fair Labor
  Standards Act and two related federal laws. The new final rule provides c
 larity to businesses and workers alike\, as well as the division’s enfor
 cement personnel.U.S. Secretary of Labor Keith Sonderling\, Wage and Hour 
 Division Administrator Andrew Rogers\, and other department officials host
 ed an event to formally announce the final rule at the Frances Perkins Bui
 lding this morning. Employers\, business associations\, worker advocates\,
  independent contractors\, and other stakeholders were in attendance as Se
 cretary Sonderling highlighted the clarity and simplicity of the new regul
 ations.The final rule rescinds an earlier 2024 rule addressing the classif
 ication of employees and independent contractors and replaces it with an a
 nalysis that is substantially similar to the framework adopted by the depa
 rtment in 2021. Consistent with the outcomes of federal appellate cases\, 
 the final rule provides further guidance on the process for differentiatin
 g between employees with protections under the FLSA and individuals who wo
 rk as independent contractors.“It’s more important than ever to ensure
  our labor regulations keep pace with the changing ways that Americans cho
 ose to work\, and businesses choose to operate\,” said Secretary Sonderl
 ing. “The new Independent Contractor final rule establishes clear\, pred
 ictable\, and easy-to-understand criteria for determining who is an employ
 ee and who is an independent contractor – allowing millions of Americans
  to take advantage of new opportunities in President Trump’s booming eco
 nomy.”The final rule also applies the department’s streamlined analysi
 s to the Family and Medical Leave Act\, and the Migrant and Seasonal Agric
 ultural Worker Protection Act\, both of which use the FLSA’s statutory d
 efinition of “employ.” However\, the rule does not chan\nhttp://www.do
 l.gov/newsroom/releases/whd/whd20261119
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/whd/whd20261119
CATEGORIES:Federal Practice Areas
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
